/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The Laplace transform converts a time function f(t) into a function of the complex variable s = σ + jω: F(s) = ∫ f(t) e−st dt, taken from t = 0⁻ to ∞.
   - Differentiation becomes multiplication.
   - F(s) is the transform of f(t); f(0⁻) is the value just before the switch; f′(0⁻) is the value of its derivative just before the switch.
   - Two theorems check the two ends of an answer without inverting it:
   - the first is the initial value theorem; the second, the final value theorem, holds only when every pole of sF(s) has a negative real part, apart from at most one pole at s = 0.
   - A resistor has no memory: v = Ri holds at every instant, so V(s) = R I(s).
   - Inductor: v = L di/dt, so V(s) = sL I(s) − L iL(0⁻).
   - Capacitor: i = C dv/dt, so I(s) = sC V(s) − C vC(0⁻), which rearranges to V(s) = (1/sC) I(s) + vC(0⁻)/s.
   - Parallel (Norton) versions swap the source for a current source: an inductor becomes sL in parallel with iL(0⁻)/s, and a capacitor becomes 1/sC in parallel with C vC(0⁻).
   - First check that the degree of the numerator is less than the degree of the denominator.
*/
