/* book exam traps: Sign and scale of the initial-condition source. An inductor needs L iL(0⁻) volts and a capacitor needs vC(0⁻)/s volts in a series branch. Writing C vC(0⁻) there is the parallel-form current source in the wrong place. The slip is silent and surfaces only at t = | Using f(0⁺) instead of f(0⁻) in the derivative rule. The theorem is stated with 0⁻ precisely because inductor current and capacitor voltage cannot jump; any other quantity can, and then the two values differ. | Applying the final value theorem to a circuit that never settles. If F(s) has a pole in the right half plane, or a pair on the jω axis as in an undamped LC, the limit does not exist and the 'steady state' you quote is fiction. | Dropping the 1/s or the ω. A constant A volts is A/s, not A, and A sin ωt is Aω/(s² + ω²), not A/(s² + ω²). A missing factor of ω is the commonest slip in the sinusoidal case. */
