/* book: lead: A network function is a ratio of one response transform to one excitation transform. Kill every independent source, set all initial energy to zero, and the circuit becomes a pure ratio H(s). That single function carries the poles that decide the natural response and the zeros that shape how the steady-state response rises or falls. Designers use it to read stability from a pole-zero plot, to get t */
/* book Figure 5.8.1: The worked example. Left: the poles of H(s) = 100/(s² + (R/L)s + 100) sit on a circle of radius ωₙ = 10 rad/s, close to the jω axis for R = 2 Ω and further left for R = 10 Ω. Right: the pole near the axis gives the tall peak of 5.03 near 9.9 rad/s; the R = 10 Ω curve peaks at only 1.15. */
