/* book exam traps: Stability comes only from poles. A zero in the right half plane does not make an unstable circuit; a pole in the right half plane does. | A pole on the jω axis is marginal, not stable. It gives sustained oscillation; any small positive real part grows without limit. | Do not forget to kill sources and zero initial conditions before forming H(s). A live source or an initial capacitor voltage adds terms that are not part of H(s). | H(jω) is not H(s) with s replaced by ω. Put s = jω, including the j. Dropping j gives the wrong sign and phase. | Cancelling a pole with a zero hides it mathematically only; exact cancellation is not robust in real components. */
